<article class="content editorial-guide">
 <p class="eyebrow">EVERYDAY WORSHIP GUIDE</p><h1>Using Islamic planning tools thoughtfully</h1>
 <p>Simple lists and counters can make routines easier to organise. They should support your own plans without claiming to judge worship, decide religious obligations or replace trusted local advice. The tools on Sunnah Tools are free and generally keep progress in your own browser.</p>
 <nav class="guide-quick-links" aria-label="Related daily tools"><a href="/tools/salah-tracker/">Daily Salah tracker ↗</a><a href="/tools/quran-planner/">Quran planner ↗</a><a href="/tools/tasbih-counter/">Digital Tasbih ↗</a><a href="/tools/umrah-packing-checklist/">Umrah packing list ↗</a></nav>
 <h2>Choose a realistic goal</h2><p>A Quran reading plan can divide pages across available days, and a Tasbih counter can tally a personal dhikr target. Adjust goals to your circumstances. Different printed mushafs have different page counts, so enter the correct total for your own copy.</p>
 <h2>Track prayer or fasting without an account</h2><p>The daily Salah checklist and fasting tracker use local browser storage. Clearing site data, changing devices or using a private window can remove saved progress. A printable prayer chart is a separate offline option for people who want a paper record.</p>
 <h2>Prepare for Hajj or Umrah</h2><p>Use the packing checklist to organise documents, essentials and travel items. Verify visa, health, airline, baggage and pilgrimage requirements with the relevant official sources near your departure, since these rules can change. The checklist is a planning aid, not an official travel requirement list.</p>
 <h2>Know what the tools cannot decide</h2><p>A tracker does not determine whether an individual owes make-up fasts or other obligations. A countdown does not confirm a moon-sighting announcement. For important religious questions, ask a qualified and trusted local authority.</p>
